and so forth. Since inference of knowledge is rejected, the usage 'it shines' must be stated to apply to knowledge precisely by virtue of its being of the nature of knowledge. And thus, the usage 'it shines' applies to objects through <s>prākaṭya</s> (manifestness), a property produced by knowledge; to knowledge by virtue of its being of the nature of knowledge;
and to the self merely by virtue of being the substrate of knowledge. Therefore, the unitary nature of [the usage 'it shines'] across all three is impossible in this view — this is established.
Having thus stated, through the teacher's position, the impossibility of unitary meaning of the usage 'prakāśate' across the knowable, knowledge, and the knower according to the <s>Bhāṭṭa</s> view, the examiner also brings out the impossibility of unitary conformity across the three even in the teacher's own view:
